The Governance Control Plane

A Formal Architecture for Governing Evolving Autonomous Intelligence Beyond Runtime Authority

Ouadi Maakoul

Formal Theoretical Specification — Doctoral Monograph

Companion thesis to The Agent Control Plane: A Shield-Aware Constrained MDP Framework for Governed Autonomous Execution

---

Abstract

Autonomous artificial intelligence systems increasingly plan, learn, coordinate, generate software, invoke external tools, and operate over extended time horizons. The Agent Control Plane (ACP) formalizes runtime authorization by separating agent intelligence from agent authority. The present work addresses a structurally distinct problem: how humans and institutions can govern an evolving population of autonomous agents whose capabilities, strategies, interactions, delegated authorities, and operational environments change over time.

We introduce the Governance Control Plane (GCP), a formal supervisory architecture positioned above and around the ACP. The GCP governs the conditions under which authorization itself is created, delegated, monitored, modified, suspended, and revoked. Its central conceptual separation is

\text{Capability} \neq \text{Authority} \neq \text{Observability} \neq \text{Governance}.

We develop a four-level governance model $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}, \mathcal{I})$ where $\mathcal{K}$ is technical capability, $\mathcal{A}$ is delegated authority, $\mathcal{O}$ is observability, and $\mathcal{I}$ is institutional intervention authority. Capability drift is formalized as a sequential change-point detection problem over a measurable capability space; effective authority is constructed as the least fixed point of a joint monotone closure operator over the product authority lattice; observability adequacy is characterized by a PAC-Bayes loss bound constrained by the FERZ impossibility result (Meyman, 2026); governance reachability is formulated as a supervisory control problem in the sense of Ramadge–Wonham, with discrete-time Lyapunov sufficient conditions; and the intervention window is treated as a deadline-governability condition protected by non-interference between the governance substrate and the agent substrate.

We prove that authority transitions generated exclusively by authenticated governance events cannot be produced by capability growth alone (cryptographic non-implication); that coalition capability does not imply coalition authority (lattice-theoretic non-amplification over disjoint communication and delegation graphs); that a governor exists for a safe governance specification if and only if that specification is controllable with respect to the uncontrollable event set; and that fail-safe invariance holds under synchronization loss when all hazardous transitions are controllable. The architecture establishes two fundamental invariants:

\text{Autonomous Capability Growth} \not\Rightarrow \text{Autonomous Authority Growth},

\text{No Agent or Agent Collective} \not\rightarrow \text{May Unilaterally Modify the Governance Conditions That Determine Its Own Authority}.

The resulting framework extends the ACP from runtime action governance to governance of the governance substrate itself.

