1. Introduction

1.1 The Second Control Problem

The Agent Control Plane establishes the foundational separation

\text{Agent Intelligence} \neq \text{Agent Authority}.

Its purpose is to prevent an agent from translating arbitrary intelligence or intent directly into consequential execution. However, long-lived autonomous systems create a second problem. Consider an agent that initially receives capability $C_0$ and delegated authority $A_0$. Over time the agent may acquire new tools, generate software, communicate with other agents, create subordinate agents, discover new strategies, modify internal policies, accumulate knowledge, operate in new environments, and exploit previously unknown paths through an environment. Its effective capability evolves,

C_t \to C_{t+1},

even when its formally delegated authority remains

A_t = A_0.

This creates a fundamental governance requirement:

C_{t+1} \nRightarrow A_{t+1}.

Capability evolution must not automatically become authority evolution. The enterprise reality confirms this gap: agent capability has become abundant across software development, deployment, and operations, while structural governability remains scarce. Organizations will only grant agents as much autonomy as they can safely observe and control (Ashley, 2026). The UASCS-1.0 standard independently establishes the requirement for structural, architectural, and legal separation between intelligence, authority, and execution within AI systems, ensuring that no intelligent system may possess implicit or emergent authority beyond explicitly assigned boundaries (Parivar, 2026).

1.2 The Four-Way Separation

We propose the following structural distinctions:

\text{Capability} \neq \text{Authority} \neq \text{Observability} \neq \text{Governance}.

A system may possess a capability without being authorized to exercise it. An action may be authorized without being adequately observable. An institution may observe an agent without possessing sufficient intervention authority. Governance therefore cannot be reduced to access control, logging, alignment, or human approval. UASCS-1.0's core principle — intelligence does not imply authority — mandates that any system capable of generating decisions must remain structurally subordinate to an external authority layer that is verifiable, independent, and cannot be overridden internally (Parivar, 2026).

1.3 Contributions

The monograph develops the following formal contributions:

1. Measurable capability space. A product capability space $\mathcal{K}$ whose components are restricted to finite or countable discrete sets per deployment domain, over which the drift metric is shown to be measurable, and therefore over which pushforward drift laws are well-defined.
2. Sequential capability drift detection. A CUSUM detector with a Lorden-optimality formulation, a two-threshold graduated escalation policy, and a Lorden/Lundberg false-alarm bound expressed in terms of the Kullback–Leibler divergence between stationary and drifting drift laws.
3. Finite-height authority lattice. An eight-facet product authority lattice with finite height, ensuring that the least fixed point of any monotone operator is reachable by finitely many Kleene iterations, and therefore computable in bounded time.
4. Effect-closure authority semantics. A closure operator on the authority lattice that separates formal authority from effective authority and characterizes zero-leakage configurations as those closed under tool effects, sub-agent effects, and communication influence.
5. PAC-Bayes observability adequacy. A bounded-loss definition of observability adequacy compatible with the FERZ impossibility result, admitting calibration guarantees under adversarial drift.
6. Ramadge–Wonham supervisory formalization. A plant/supervisor model of the ACP/GCP interface, with controllability characterization of safe governance specifications and fail-safe invariance under synchronization loss.
7. Non-interference of the intervention window. A formal isolation condition under which adversarial agent strategies cannot degrade detection or intervention latency, thereby rendering deadline governability robust to the agent's strategy.
8. Cryptographic governance integrity. Formal theorems establishing that capability growth cannot produce authority growth, and that agents cannot unilaterally modify the governance substrate, under explicit cryptographic assumptions with epoch binding, key rotation, and policy-hash binding.
9. Coalition non-amplification. A lattice-theoretic theorem over disjoint communication and delegation graphs, establishing that emergent multi-agent coordination cannot produce coalition authority absent explicit signed delegation.
10. Five-plane reference architecture. A layered architecture with a signed governance event bus, Byzantine-fault-tolerant event ordering, Merkle-consistency governance receipts, and a fail-safe ACP synchronization protocol.

1.4 Methodological Stance

Rapid AI capability growth is used throughout this monograph not as a prediction about a specific future date, but as a stress-test condition. The scientific question is not whether a particular capability timeline will occur, but whether the proposed governance architecture remains structurally valid if autonomous capability improves much faster than institutional adaptation. A governance architecture should remain meaningful even when the forecast that motivated the stress test is wrong. Consequently, the empirical benchmarks drawn from the 2026 literature (GAAT, LATTICE) are used as proposed verification conditions — bounds that a deployment must meet for the intervention-window condition to hold — rather than as executed physical measurements. The theorems are mathematical; the architecture is a specification; the evaluation framework is a falsifiable protocol.

