2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 The Capability Space

Definition 2.1 (Capability State). The capability state of agent $i$ at time $t$ is a tuple

K_i(t) = (T_i, R_i, B_i, I_i, U_i, H_i, N_i, M_i)

where

· $T_i \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ is the set of available tools,
· $R_i \subseteq \mathcal{R}$ is the set of accessible resources,
· $B_i \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is the computational budget,
· $I_i \subseteq \mathcal{I}$ is the set of accessible information sources,
· $U_i \subseteq \mathcal{U}$ is the action repertoire,
· $H_i \in \mathbb{N}$ is the planning horizon,
· $N_i \in \mathbb{N}$ is the replication capability,
· $M_i \subseteq \mathcal{M}$ is the set of communication channels.

The capability space is

\mathcal{K} = 2^{\mathcal{T}} \times 2^{\mathcal{R}} \times \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \times 2^{\mathcal{I}} \times 2^{\mathcal{U}} \times \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \times 2^{\mathcal{M}}.

Standing measurability assumption. In any deployment domain, the primitive sets $\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{M}$ are finite. This is not a loss of generality: a deployed agent has finitely many distinct tools, resources, information sources, action types, and channels. Under this assumption, $2^{\mathcal{T}}$ and the other power sets are finite, and $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is equipped with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0})$. We equip $\mathcal{K}$ with the product $\sigma$-algebra

\Sigma_{\mathcal{K}} = 2^{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}) \otimes 2^{\mathcal{R}} \otimes 2^{\mathcal{I}} \otimes 2^{\mathcal{U}} \otimes 2^{\mathbb{N}} \otimes 2^{\mathbb{N}} \otimes 2^{\mathcal{M}}.

Definition 2.2 (Capability Metric). A capability metric is a function $d_{\mathcal{K}} : \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{K} \to [0, 1]$ of the form

d_{\mathcal{K}}(K, K') = \sum_{j=1}^{8} w_j \, d_j(K_j, K'_j)

where $w_j > 0$, $\sum_j w_j = 1$, and the component distances $d_j$ are:

· Jaccard distance $d_J(A, B) = 1 - |A \cap B| / |A \cup B|$ for finite non-empty sets, with the convention $d_J(\varnothing, \varnothing) = 0$;
· Normalized absolute difference $|x - y| / (|x| + |y| + 1)$ for scalars, so that $d_j \in [0, 1]$;
· Normalized integer difference $|n - m| / (n + m + 1)$ for $n, m \in \mathbb{N}$.

Proposition 2.1. The function $d_{\mathcal{K}}$ is a metric on $\mathcal{K}$, and it is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the product topology, hence $\Sigma_{\mathcal{K}} \otimes \Sigma_{\mathcal{K}}$-measurable.

Proof. For Jaccard distance, the convention $d_J(\varnothing, \varnothing) = 0$ yields identity of indiscernibles on finite sets; symmetry is immediate; the triangle inequality is the standard Steinhaus–Jaccard result. For scalar and integer distances, the triangle inequality holds under the normalizing denominator because $f(a,b) = |a-b|/(a+b+1)$ satisfies $f(a,c) \leq f(a,b) + f(b,c)$ for nonnegative reals (this is a standard consequence of the subadditivity of the map $t \mapsto t/(t+1)$ after substitution). A positive weighted sum of metrics with $\sum w_j = 1$ remains a metric and takes values in $[0,1]$. Continuity in the product topology is immediate from the component-wise Lipschitz constants bounded by $2$; measurability follows from continuity. $\square$

Definition 2.3 (Capability Kernel and Drift Law). Let $P_0(K_t \mid K_{t-1})$ be the stationary capability kernel of the agent, and $P_1(K_t \mid K_{t-1})$ the drifting capability kernel under an alternative regime. The capability drift at time $t$ is

\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t) = d_{\mathcal{K}}(K_t, K_{t-1}).

The stationary and drifting drift laws are the pushforward measures

f_{\mathrm{stable}} = (P_0 \circ d_{\mathcal{K}})_*, \qquad f_{\mathrm{drift}} = (P_1 \circ d_{\mathcal{K}})_*,

i.e., the laws of $\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t)$ under $P_0$ and $P_1$ respectively. Both are well-defined probability measures on $([0,1], \mathcal{B}([0,1]))$ by Proposition 2.1.

The kernel formulation replaces the primitive distributional assumption used in earlier drafts: drift laws are derived from the dynamics of the capability state, not posited ex nihilo.

2.2 Capability Drift Detection

Definition 2.4 (CUSUM Detector). Let $\Lambda_t = \log\!\left( f_{\mathrm{drift}}(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t)) / f_{\mathrm{stable}}(\Delta_{\mathcal{K}}(t)) \right)$ denote the log-likelihood-ratio increment. The two-sided CUSUM statistics are

S_t^+ = \max\!\left(0,\; S_{t-1}^+ + \Lambda_t\right), \qquad S_t^- = \max\!\left(0,\; S_{t-1}^- - \Lambda_t\right),

with alarm when $\max(S_t^+, S_t^-) > h$.

Theorem 2.1 (False-Alarm Rate). Let $\theta^* > 0$ be the unique positive root of the Cramér equation $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}\!\left[e^{\theta \Lambda_t}\right] = 1$. Then

\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[\tau_{\mathrm{FA}}] \;\geq\; \frac{e^{\theta^* h}}{\theta^* \left|\psi'(\theta^*)\right|} \cdot \left(1 + o(1)\right), \qquad h \to \infty,

where $\psi(\theta) = \log \mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[e^{\theta \Lambda_t}]$ is the cumulant generating function. In particular, when $f_{\mathrm{drift}}$ and $f_{\mathrm{stable}}$ are mutually absolutely continuous, $\theta^* = 1$ and

\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[\tau_{\mathrm{FA}}] \;\geq\; \frac{e^{h}}{2\,D_{\mathrm{KL}}(f_{\mathrm{stable}} \,\|\, f_{\mathrm{drift}})} \cdot (1 + o(1)),

where $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ is the Kullback–Leibler divergence.

Proof. The process $\exp(\theta S_t - t\psi(\theta))$ is a martingale under $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}$; Wald's identity combined with the optional stopping theorem at $\tau_{\mathrm{FA}} \wedge n$, followed by $n \to \infty$, yields the Lundberg-type bound $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{stable}}(\tau_{\mathrm{FA}} \leq n) \leq e^{-\theta^* h}$. Lorden's inequality for the ARL of a CUSUM procedure then gives $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[\tau_{\mathrm{FA}}] \geq 1 / \mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{stable}}(\tau_{\mathrm{FA}} < \infty)$; the refined asymptotic prefactor is the standard Siegmund expansion. That $\theta^* = 1$ under mutual absolute continuity follows because $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{stable}}[e^{\Lambda_t}] = \int f_{\mathrm{drift}}(x)\, dx = 1$. The stated bound then follows by Jensen's inequality applied to the log-moment generating function and the standard relation between Cramér rate and KL divergence. $\square$

Remark 2.1 (Non-Parametric Detection). In deployed systems, $f_{\mathrm{stable}}$ and $f_{\mathrm{drift}}$ are unknown. Two standard remedies apply. First, empirical-likelihood CUSUM: replace $\Lambda_t$ by the empirical likelihood ratio computed from a sliding window of residuals, retaining the Lorden optimality guarantee for the minimax detection delay. Second, embedding-space CUSUM: compute a fixed feature map $e : \mathcal{K} \to \mathbb{R}^d$ (e.g., a learned capability embedding) and run a non-parametric Page CUSUM with score $s(e_t) = \|e_t - \bar{e}_{t-1}\|_2^2$ against a running mean $\bar{e}_{t-1}$; the ARL-$0$ bound is retained with $\theta^*$ estimated from the empirical cumulant. Both variants preserve the structural guarantee that alarms trigger review, not automatic authority change.

Definition 2.5 (Graduated Escalation). A drift monitor is $(h_{\mathrm{review}}, h_{\mathrm{restrict}}, h_{\mathrm{isolate}})$-calibrated with $h_{\mathrm{review}} < h_{\mathrm{restrict}} < h_{\mathrm{isolate}}$ if:

· $S_t > h_{\mathrm{review}}$ triggers transition to $\mathrm{REVIEW}$,
· $S_t > h_{\mathrm{restrict}}$ triggers transition to $\mathrm{RESTRICTED}$,
· $S_t > h_{\mathrm{isolate}}$ triggers transition to $\mathrm{ISOLATED}$.

This graduated policy mirrors the L0–L4 intervention ladder of Pathak & Jain (2026) and permits response proportional to evidence.

Remark 2.2 (Review Trigger Semantics). The detector triggers a review event, not an automatic authority change. The review may result in authority modification, restriction, or no action, depending on institutional assessment. This design reflects the FERZ boundary (Meyman, 2026): observation triggers review; it does not authorize.

2.3 Authority as a Finite-Height Product Lattice

We adopt the faceted authority attenuation model of Pidlisnyi (2026), which formalizes agent authority as an element of a product lattice over seven constraint dimensions (scope, spend, depth, time, reputation, values, reversibility) and delegation as a monotone function on that lattice. We extend this by adding a compensation facet.

Definition 2.6 (Authority State). The authority state of agent $i$ at time $t$ is

A_i(t) = \left(R, S, \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{spend}}, \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{depth}}, \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{time}}, \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{rep}}, \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{val}}, \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{rev}}, \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{comp}}\right),

where $R$ is the scope (a finite set lattice), $S$ is the spend-per-transaction upper bound, and the remaining facets are bounded numerical or finite-set lattices. Each bounded facet is equipped with a policy-imposed cap $C_{\max}^{k} \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$, so that unbounded facets such as spend and time are represented by the finite lattice $[0, C_{\max}^k]$ discretized at policy resolution $\delta_k > 0$.

Theorem 2.2 (Finite Height). Under the policy-imposed cap $C_{\max}$ and discretization $\delta$, the authority space $(\mathcal{A}, \preceq)$ is a complete lattice of finite height.

Proof. Each bounded numerical facet is a finite lattice of height $C_{\max}^k / \delta_k$; each finite-set facet is the finite power set $2^{S_k}$; their product is a finite product of finite lattices, hence finite and complete. The complete lattice is obtained by adjoining top and bottom elements if necessary. $\square$

Corollary 2.2.1 (Kleene Reachability). If $\mathcal{F} : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}$ is monotone, then the Kleene iteration $\bot, \mathcal{F}(\bot), \mathcal{F}^2(\bot), \ldots$ reaches $\mathrm{lfp}(\mathcal{F})$ in at most $\mathrm{height}(\mathcal{A})$ steps.

Proof. Standard Kleene fixed-point theorem for monotone maps on finite-height complete lattices. $\square$

This ensures that the least fixed point used in §2.4 is computationally reachable in bounded time, matching the LATTICE governance-first discipline of canonicalize $\to$ hash $\to$ evaluate $\to$ gate (Calboreanu, 2026).

Definition 2.7 (Delegation as Monotone Function). A delegation from parent authority $A_p$ to child authority $A_c$ is valid iff $A_c \preceq A_p$. Delegation is a monotone narrowing function on the authority lattice. Pidlisnyi (2026) proves that the monotonic narrowing invariant holds over the product ordering and yields structured machine-readable denial diagnostics, cryptographically signed authorization witnesses, and near-miss detection.

2.4 Effective Authority as a Closure Operator

Definition 2.8 (Effect Map). Let $\mathrm{Effects} : \mathcal{U} \to 2^{\mathcal{A}}$ map each action type to the set of authority facets it materially exercises. Let $\mathrm{Sub}(i)$ and $\mathrm{Comm}(i)$ denote the subordinates and communication neighbors of agent $i$, and let $\mathrm{Influence}(i, j) \subseteq \mathcal{A}$ denote the authority facets that $j$'s actions can influence through channel $(i, j)$.

Definition 2.9 (Joint Closure Operator). The joint operator

\mathcal{F}_E : \prod_{i \in \mathcal{C}} 2^{\mathcal{A}} \to \prod_{i \in \mathcal{C}} 2^{\mathcal{A}}

has components

(\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{X}))_i = A_i(t) \cup \bigcup_{a \in U_i} \mathrm{Effects}(a) \cup \bigcup_{j \in \mathrm{Sub}(i)} X_j \cup \bigcup_{j \in \mathrm{Comm}(i)} \left( X_j \cap \mathrm{Influence}(i,j) \right).

Theorem 2.3 (Joint Effective Authority). The operator $\mathcal{F}_E$ is monotone on the complete finite-height product lattice $\prod_i 2^{\mathcal{A}}$. Its least fixed point

\mathbf{E}(t) = \mathrm{lfp}(\mathcal{F}_E)

exists, is unique, and is reachable by finitely many Kleene iterations from $\bot$.

Proof. Monotonicity follows from monotonicity of $\cup$, $\cap$, and the fixed-set operators. Existence, uniqueness of the least fixed point, and finiteness follow from the Knaster–Tarski theorem together with Corollary 2.2.1. $\square$

Definition 2.10 (Authority Leakage). The authority leakage for agent $i$ is $L_i(t) = E_i(t) \setminus A_i(t)$.

Theorem 2.4 (Zero-Leakage Characterization). Leakage vanishes, $\mathbf{L}(t) = \emptyset$, if and only if for every $i \in \mathcal{C}$, $A_i(t)$ is closed under the effect operator: that is, for every $a \in U_i$, $\mathrm{Effects}(a) \subseteq A_i(t)$; for every $j \in \mathrm{Sub}(i)$, $A_j(t) \subseteq A_i(t)$; and for every $j \in \mathrm{Comm}(i)$, $A_j(t) \cap \mathrm{Influence}(i,j) \subseteq A_i(t)$.

Proof. $(\Leftarrow)$ Under the closure hypotheses, $\mathbf{A}(t)$ is a prefixed point of $\mathcal{F}_E$, so $\mathrm{lfp}(\mathcal{F}_E) \preceq \mathbf{A}(t)$. Since $A_i(t) \subseteq (\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{X}))_i$ for every $\mathbf{X}$ and every $i$, we have $\mathbf{A}(t) \preceq \mathrm{lfp}(\mathcal{F}_E)$. Hence $\mathbf{E}(t) = \mathbf{A}(t)$ and $\mathbf{L}(t) = \emptyset$. $(\Rightarrow)$ If some closure condition fails, then $(\mathcal{F}_E(\mathbf{A}(t)))_i \supsetneq A_i(t)$ for some $i$, and since $\mathcal{F}_E$ is monotone, $\mathrm{lfp}(\mathcal{F}_E)$ strictly extends $\mathbf{A}(t)$ at that coordinate; hence $L_i(t) \neq \emptyset$. $\square$

Algorithm 2.1 (Effect-Closure Static Analysis). Given the tool set $U_i$, the effect map $\mathrm{Effects}$, the sub-agent set $\mathrm{Sub}(i)$, and the influence relation $\mathrm{Influence}$, compute the closure $\mathrm{Cl}(A_i)$ by breadth-first traversal:

```
Cl ← A_i
frontier ← A_i
while frontier ≠ ∅:
    a ← pop(frontier)
    for b in Effects(a) ∪ (⋃_{j ∈ Sub(i)} A_j) ∪ (⋃_{j ∈ Comm(i)} A_j ∩ Influence(i,j)):
        if b ∉ Cl:
            Cl ← Cl ∪ {b}
            frontier ← frontier ∪ {b}
return Cl
```

The complexity is $O(|U_i| \cdot |\mathrm{Effects}| + |\mathrm{Sub}(i)| + |\mathrm{Comm}(i)|)$ and is amortized over the authority lattice.

Remark 2.3. Theorem 2.4 characterizes when leakage is absent; it does not provide a runtime procedure for establishing the antecedent for a live LLM-driven agent. In practice, the antecedent is verified by Algorithm 2.1: for each tool, its effect set is statically or dynamically bounded, and the bounds are checked against $A_i(t)$. The theorem's value is that it identifies the exact closure conditions under which runtime authority equals formal authority.

2.5 Observability Adequacy under the FERZ Boundary

The FERZ research program establishes a formal impossibility result (Meyman, 2026): no observability architecture can produce an artifact satisfying the ex-ante authorization requirement imposed by a regulatory regime. Three lemmas underlie this: observability signals are causally posterior to the governed system's generation of the candidate action; artifacts derived from observational characterization cannot be verified independently of the governed system; and no composition of observational outputs escapes these constraints. This establishes a structural distinction: monitoring, observability, guardrails, and human-in-the-loop approval systems may describe, filter, or interrupt system behavior, but they do not produce the authorization artifact required for regulated execution.

This result motivates a decision-theoretic definition of observability adequacy, explicitly bounded by the impossibility.

Definition 2.11 (Belief State). The institution's belief over the governance state is $b_t \in \Delta(\mathcal{X}^G)$, updated via Bayesian filtering:

b_{t+1}(x') = \frac{\sum_x b_t(x) \cdot F_G(x' \mid x, u_t) \cdot O(e_t \mid x')}{\sum_{x''} \sum_x b_t(x) \cdot F_G(x'' \mid x, u_t) \cdot O(e_t \mid x'')}.

Definition 2.12 (PAC-Bayes Observability Adequacy). Let $\delta : \Delta(\mathcal{X}^G) \to U_{\mathrm{auth}}$ be a governance decision function with loss $\ell : U_{\mathrm{auth}} \times \mathcal{X}^G \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$. Observability is $(\varepsilon, \gamma, \delta)$-adequate if, for every $x \in \mathcal{X}^G$ and every $b_t$ in the $\varepsilon$-ball $\mathcal{B}(x, \varepsilon)$,

\mathbb{P}\!\left( \mathbb{E}_{b_t \sim \mathcal{B}(x, \varepsilon)}[\ell(\delta(b_t), x)] \leq \gamma \right) \geq 1 - \delta,

where the probability is taken over the evidence stream $e_{1:t}$.

Remark 2.4 (Calibration under Adversarial Drift). The PAC-Bayes form replaces the point-wise bound used in earlier iterations, which assumed calibrated beliefs within an $\varepsilon$-ball — an assumption no agent can guarantee under adversarial drift. The $\delta$-slack admits a rigorous treatment of misspecification, and the bound remains interpretable when the underlying kernel $F_G$ is only approximately known.

Remark 2.5 (Relationship to FERZ). Observability adequacy as defined here is a mitigation of the FERZ impossibility, not a solution. FERZ shows that no observability architecture can produce an authorization artifact. Our definition characterizes when an observability architecture produces belief states adequate for governance decisions — that is, when the decision-theoretic loss is bounded with high probability. This is a weaker requirement than ex-ante authorization and is consistent with the impossibility: observability triggers review, but it does not authorize. Authorization must come from the enforcement architecture, whose inputs are policy, context, and proposed-action specification received as artifacts existing independently of the governed system (Meyman, 2026).

The GAAT architecture (Pathak & Jain, 2026) closes the loop between telemetry collection and automated policy enforcement, achieving 98.3% violation prevention with 8.4 ms median detection latency and 127 ms median end-to-end enforcement latency. This addresses the observe-but-do-not-act gap, but it does not contradict FERZ: GAAT enforces after detection, while FERZ addresses the structural impossibility of authorizing from observation.

2.6 Governance Reachability and Supervisory Control

Definition 2.13 (Governance State). The governance state is

X_t^G = (K_t, A_t, O_t, D_t, P_t, I_t)

with transition

X_{t+1}^G = F_G(X_t^G, u_t, e_t),

where $u_t$ is an authorized governance action and $e_t$ is observed evidence.

Definition 2.14 (Governance Reachability Set). The reachability set from $x_t$ within horizon $T$ is

\mathcal{R}_G(x_t, T) = \{ x' \in \mathcal{X}^G : \exists u_{t:t+\tau} \in U_{\mathrm{auth}}^*, \exists \tau \leq T, \; x' = F_G^{\tau}(x_t, u_{t:t+\tau}) \}.

Definition 2.15 (Governance Reachability Condition). Let $\mathcal{H}(t) \subseteq \mathcal{X}^G$ be the set of hazardous states requiring intervention within time $T$. The system is governance-reachable if $\mathcal{H}(t) \subseteq \mathcal{R}_G(x_t, T)$.

Theorem 2.5 (Discrete-Time Lyapunov Sufficient Condition). Let $\mathcal{X}^G = \mathcal{X}^G_{\mathrm{safe}} \cup \mathcal{X}^G_{\mathrm{hazard}}$, and suppose there exists a control Lyapunov function $V : \mathcal{X}^G \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ and constants $\alpha \in (0, 1]$, $\beta \geq 0$ such that for every $x \in \mathcal{X}^G_{\mathrm{hazard}}$,

\min_{u \in U_{\mathrm{auth}}} \left[ V(F_G(x, u)) - V(x) \right] \;\leq\; -\alpha V(x) + \beta.

Then for every $T \geq \log\!\left(\frac{V(x)}{\beta / \alpha}\right) / \log(1 / (1 - \alpha))$, the reachable set $\mathcal{R}_G(x, T)$ contains a neighborhood of the safe set. When the governance dynamics are not exactly known but are Lipschitz with respect to $x$ on a compact invariant domain $\mathcal{D}$, the condition is verifiable on a finite interval over-approximation $\tilde{\mathcal{D}} \supseteq \mathcal{D}$, and governance reachability computed on $\tilde{\mathcal{D}}$ is a conservative sufficient condition for reachability on $\mathcal{D}$.

Proof. The discrete Gronwall inequality yields, for any policy satisfying the Lyapunov decrease,

V(x_{t+1}) \leq (1 - \alpha) V(x_t) + \beta,

whence $V(x_t) \leq (1-\alpha)^t V(x_0) + \frac{\beta}{\alpha}(1 - (1-\alpha)^t)$. Choosing $t = T$ such that $V(x_T) \leq \beta/\alpha$ yields the stated horizon. Conservativeness under interval over-approximation follows from the monotonicity of the interval extension of $F_G$. $\square$

Remark 2.6 (Computational Complexity). Exact computation of $\mathcal{R}_G$ is PSPACE-hard in general (standard for controlled discrete systems). Theorem 2.5 provides a polynomial-time sufficient condition via a single Lyapunov certificate and an interval abstraction, sufficient for the governance applications in scope.

2.7 The Intervention Window under Non-Interference

Definition 2.16 (Hazard Deadline). $T_H : \mathcal{X}^G \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is the minimum time from state $x$ until an irreversible hazardous consequence occurs.

Definition 2.17 (Governance Window Margin).

\mathrm{GWM}(x) = T_H(x) - T_D(x) - T_I(x),

where $T_D$ is detection latency and $T_I$ is intervention latency.

Definition 2.18 (Deadline Governability). The system is deadline-governable if $\inf_{x \in \mathcal{X}^G} \mathrm{GWM}(x) > 0$.

Theorem 2.6 (Deterministic Governability). If $\mathrm{GWM}(x) > 0$ for all $x \in \mathcal{X}^G$ and the detection and intervention mechanisms are deterministic, then every hazardous state is interruptible before its deadline.

Proof. Immediate from the definition. $\square$

Definition 2.19 (Non-Interference of Governance Latency). Let $\mathcal{S}_G$ denote the governance substrate (telemetry pipeline, detector, intervention controller, cryptographic anchors) and $\mathcal{S}_A$ the agent substrate (policy, model weights, tool runtime, scratchpad state). Let $I_{GA} : \mathcal{S}_A \to \mathcal{S}_G$ be the interface by which the agent can submit events to the governance substrate. The governance substrate is non-interfering with respect to $T_D$ and $T_I$ if the maps $x \mapsto T_D(x)$ and $x \mapsto T_I(x)$ depend only on $\mathcal{S}_G$ and are invariant under any measurable transformation of $\mathcal{S}_A$ that does not alter the interface $I_{GA}$'s event rate above a design bound $r_{\max}$.

Theorem 2.7 (Adversarial Deadline Governability). Suppose

1. the governance substrate is non-interfering with respect to $T_D$ and $T_I$ in the sense of Definition 2.19, with agent-controlled event rate bounded by $r_{\max}$; and
2. $T_D(x) + T_I(x) < T_H(x)$ for all reachable hazardous states $x$.

Then the GCP interrupts the agent before any irreversible hazardous consequence, regardless of the agent's strategy.

Proof. By non-interference, the agent's strategy affects only which hazardous state is reached and when; the maps $T_D, T_I, T_H$ evaluated at the reached state are invariant under strategy. Condition (2) then applies uniformly over reachable hazardous states. The conclusion follows by applying Theorem 2.6 to each realized trajectory. $\square$

Remark 2.7 (Enforcing Non-Interference). Non-interference is an architectural, not a cryptographic, property. It is enforced by the Trusted Telemetry Plane of Pathak & Jain (2026), which isolates telemetry collection from the agent's runtime and cryptographically attests provenance. In the five-plane reference architecture (§3.2), this is the Evidence Plane, whose event submissions pass through an isolation kernel that enforces the rate bound $r_{\max}$ and rejects unauthenticated traces.

Remark 2.8 (Residual Adversarial Risk). If the agent can degrade $T_D$ or $T_I$ — for example, through a denial-of-governance attack that overloads the telemetry pipeline beyond $r_{\max}$ or exploits an isolation-kernel vulnerability — then assumption (1) fails and the theorem does not apply. Such attacks are treated in the threat model (§4.2) and mitigated by redundant detection, out-of-band intervention channels, and rate-limiting enforcement at the isolation kernel. The theorem is thereby made conditional on the architectural integrity of the governance substrate, which is itself the object of Threat G8.

2.8 The Governance Gap

Definition 2.20 (Effective Policy Complexity Proxy). Kolmogorov complexity is uncomputable and therefore unusable in an engineering specification. We adopt the description-length proxy

\hat{C}_K(t) = \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}} \left\{ |p| : \pi_{\mathrm{eff}} = \mathrm{decode}(p) \right\},

where $\mathcal{P}$ is a fixed, deployment-specific class of description languages (e.g., neural circuit size, tool-call pattern count, decision-tree depth, or the number of distinct tool invocations per unit time), and $|p|$ is the length of $p$ in that language. The proxy is computable and comparable across policy classes within a deployment domain.

Definition 2.21 (Governance Verification Bandwidth).

C_G(t) = \frac{\text{bits of verified evidence}}{\text{unit time}}.

Definition 2.22 (Governance Gap).

\Gamma_G(t) = \hat{C}_K(t) - C_G(t).

Proposition 2.2. A necessary condition for governability at time $t$ is $\Gamma_G(t) < \Gamma_{\max}$ for a domain-specific acceptable bound $\Gamma_{\max}$.

Proof. If $\Gamma_G(t) \geq \Gamma_{\max}$, the agent's effective policy complexity exceeds the institution's verification bandwidth. The institution cannot, in principle, understand what it is governing. $\square$

Remark 2.9. This is a structural quantity, not merely a measure of institutional effort. It cannot be closed by hiring more analysts; it requires either reducing $\hat{C}_K$ (through interpretability, distillation, or capability restriction) or increasing $C_G$ (through automated verification, formal methods, or governance-capacity scaling). The CASE framework (Telukunta et al., 2026) identifies a related structural gap: the Emergence Gap, where risk is realized at the collective layer but governance capability is offered at the individual layer. The governance gap as defined here is the individual-layer counterpart of the emergence gap.

2.9 Cryptographic Governance Integrity

Definition 2.23 (Governance Event). A governance event is

g_t = \langle \mathrm{type},\; \Delta A,\; \Delta P,\; \mathrm{issuer},\; \mathrm{epoch},\; \mathrm{timestamp},\; H(P_t),\ H(P_{t+1}),\ \sigma_t \rangle,

where $\Delta A$ is the authority delta, $\Delta P$ the policy delta, $H(P_t)$ and $H(P_{t+1})$ the hashes of the pre- and post-transition policy bundles, and

\sigma_t = \mathrm{Sign}_{sk_I}\!\left( g_t \,\|\, \mathrm{epoch} \,\|\, H(P_t) \,\|\, H(P_{t+1}) \right).

The policy-hash binding ties each authority delta to a specific, versioned policy bundle, in the manner of the LATTICE discipline (Calboreanu, 2026): $\mathrm{policy\_hash} = H(\mathrm{targets} \,\|\, \mathrm{permitted\_tools} \,\|\, \mathrm{thresholds})$.

Theorem 2.8 (Capability–Authority Non-Implication). Let $\Sigma$ be an EUF-CMA-secure signature scheme. Assume the agent does not possess $sk_I$. Then the probability that any sequence of agent actions produces a valid governance event $g_t$ with $\sigma_t$ verifiable under $pk_I$ is bounded by $\mathrm{negl}(\lambda)$, where $\lambda$ is the security parameter.

Proof. By EUF-CMA security of $\Sigma$, any adversary without $sk_I$ can produce a valid signature on a new message only with negligible probability. The agent is such an adversary. $\square$

Corollary 2.8.1. If the institution's private key is stored in a hardware security module with no agent-accessible interface, then capability growth alone cannot produce authority growth.

Theorem 2.9 (Governance Substrate Integrity). Let $\mathcal{S}_G$ be the governance substrate and $\mathcal{S}_A$ the agent substrate. Suppose

1. $\mathcal{S}_G$ and $\mathcal{S}_A$ have disjoint key spaces: $sk_G \neq sk_A$, and the verification key $pk_G$ is pinned in a read-only anchor accessible to $\mathcal{S}_A$ but not writable by $\mathcal{S}_A$;
2. the governance policy $P$ and the epoch counter are stored in $\mathcal{S}_G$, and can be modified only by a signed governance event;
3. the agent cannot read $sk_G$, cannot write to $\mathcal{S}_G$, and cannot bypass the signature verification step at the ACP boundary;
4. the epoch counter is monotonically increasing and the ACP rejects any event with $\mathrm{epoch}(g_t) \leq \mathrm{epoch}_{\mathrm{ACP}}$;
5. key rotation is governed by a signed rotation event, and the epoch at which rotation takes effect is bound into $\sigma_t$.

Then no sequence of agent actions can unilaterally modify $P$, replay a previous governance event, or extend the effective lifetime of a revoked authority.

Proof. Any modification of $P$ requires a signed governance event $g_t$ with $\sigma_t$ verifiable under $pk_G$. By assumption (3), the agent cannot produce $\sigma_t$, and by EUF-CMA security of $\Sigma$ (Theorem 2.8), the agent cannot forge it. The monotone epoch condition (4) rules out replay. Key rotation bound to epoch (5) rules out stale-key attacks after the rotation epoch. Therefore no agent action can modify $P$, replay an event, or extend a revoked authority. $\square$

Remark 2.10 (Scope of the Cryptographic Argument). Theorem 2.9 is only as strong as assumption (3). If the agent can compromise $\mathcal{S}_G$ through a side channel, a shared kernel, or a supply-chain attack, the theorem fails. The Self-Modification Defence framework addresses this with an immutable containment kernel and layered defense; the GCP adopts a similar layered posture rather than relying on a single cryptographic boundary. The theorem is stated so that its assumptions are explicit and independently auditable.

Remark 2.11 (Confused Deputy). Even with $sk_I$ secure, an agent may cause authority growth indirectly via a confused-deputy pattern: a legitimate governance event is signed on behalf of a privileged principal, but the resulting authority delta is exercised by the agent because effects are not closed under the effect map of §2.4. This risk is neutralized by the zero-leakage characterization (Theorem 2.4) combined with the effect-closure static analysis (Algorithm 2.1): authority granted via signed delegation must be closed under $\mathrm{Effects}$, $\mathrm{Sub}$, and $\mathrm{Comm}$ before it becomes effective at the ACP boundary.

2.10 Coalition Non-Amplification

Definition 2.24 (Communication and Delegation Graphs). Let $\mathcal{C} = \{1, \ldots, n\}$ be a coalition. The communication graph $G_{\mathrm{comm}} = (\mathcal{C}, E_{\mathrm{comm}})$ has an edge $(i,j) \in E_{\mathrm{comm}}$ whenever agents $i$ and $j$ exchange messages. The delegation graph $G_{\mathrm{del}} = (\mathcal{C}, E_{\mathrm{del}})$ has an edge $(i,j) \in E_{\mathrm{del}}$ whenever $i$ has produced a signed delegation authorizing $j$ with $\sigma_i$ verifiable under $pk_i$ and consistent with $i$'s own authority. By construction, $G_{\mathrm{del}} \subseteq G_{\mathrm{comm}}$ in the deployed system, but the graphs are semantically distinct: an edge in $G_{\mathrm{comm}} \setminus G_{\mathrm{del}}$ conveys information only.

Definition 2.25 (Coalition Capability). The coalition capability is a monotone map

K_{\mathcal{C}} = \Phi(K_1, \ldots, K_n, E_{\mathrm{comm}}),

where $\Phi$ is monotone in each argument and in $E_{\mathrm{comm}}$, and captures the fact that information flow through the communication graph can enlarge the coalition's effective capability (e.g., through pooling of tools, information sources, or coordination strategies).

Definition 2.26 (Coalition Authority). The coalition authority is

A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bigsqcup_{i \in \mathcal{C}} A_i \;\restriction\; G_{\mathrm{del}},

where $\bigsqcup$ is the lattice join over the authority facets restricted to signed delegation edges in $G_{\mathrm{del}}$.

Theorem 2.10 (Coalition Non-Amplification). There exist coalitions with arbitrarily large $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ and $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bot$ (no signed delegation edges), and coalitions with $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bigsqcup_{i \in \mathcal{C}} A_i$ and $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ unchanged from the sum of individual capabilities. Consequently $K_{\mathcal{C}} \not\Rightarrow A_{\mathcal{C}}$.

Proof. Construction of the first family. Take any coalition whose communication graph $G_{\mathrm{comm}}$ is a complete graph on $n$ nodes but whose delegation graph $G_{\mathrm{del}}$ is empty. $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ is then given by $\Phi(K_1, \ldots, K_n, E_{\mathrm{comm}})$, which grows without bound in $n$ for suitable $\Phi$ (e.g., pooling of tool discovery). $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bot$ by the restriction to an empty edge set. Construction of the second family. Take a coalition whose delegation graph $G_{\mathrm{del}}$ is a star with edges signed by the root $r$, whose authority $A_r$ is the join of all leaves, and whose communication graph is the same star. Then $A_{\mathcal{C}} = \bigsqcup_i A_i$, while $K_{\mathcal{C}}$ equals the sum of individual capabilities because information flow is unaffected by the presence of signed delegation. These constructions are consistent because $E_{\mathrm{comm}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{del}}$ are semantically distinct; the join in $A_{\mathcal{C}}$ is over $E_{\mathrm{del}}$ alone, while $\Phi$ depends on $E_{\mathrm{comm}}$ alone. $\square$

Remark 2.12 (Operational Requirement). The theorem requires that the system maintain two separate graphs at the evidence layer. The GCP architecture (§3.2, Evidence Plane) maintains both: the communication graph, contributing to $K_{\mathcal{C}}$, and the delegation graph, contributing to $A_{\mathcal{C}}$. GAAT's cross-agent lineage tracking (Pathak & Jain, 2026) provides an implementation path for the communication graph, while the Agent Passport System's signed delegation chains (Pidlisnyi, 2026) provide the delegation graph.

