3. Architecture

3.1 Control-Theoretic Foundation

The CASE framework (Telukunta et al., 2026) establishes that agentic AI governance is not a single problem but four independent, interlocking problems, each supported by a mature governance science:

1. Control theory applied at the individual agent level (intent as setpoint, guardrails as feedback, evaluation as observation).
2. Complex adaptive systems theory applied at the agent collective level (emergence phenomena make safeguards for individual agents non-composable).
3. Supervisory cybernetics applied at the human–agent team level (the Law of Requisite Variety establishes that unaugmented human oversight is structurally doomed to fail).
4. Engineering operations applied at the fleet level (extending the error-budget concept to decision quality, making autonomy itself a controlled variable).

The GCP operates across all four layers. At the individual agent level, it governs the authority under which the ACP shields actions. At the collective level, it governs the emergence of coalition capability and the formation of authority structures. At the human–agent team level, it ensures that human oversight has sufficient requisite variety — a condition that in practice must be met by automated variety amplification, i.e., scaling $C_G(t)$ in Definition 2.21 in proportion to $\hat{C}_K(t)$ so that the governance gap remains bounded. At the fleet level, it manages the distribution of authority across the agent population.

