Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations

A.1 The Measurable Capability Space

A.1.1 Standing Finiteness Assumption.

The primitive sets $\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{M}$ are finite in every deployment domain. This is not a restriction but a reflection of deployed reality: a deployed agent has finitely many distinct tools, resources, information sources, action types, and communication channels. Under this assumption, the power sets $2^{\mathcal{T}}, 2^{\mathcal{R}}, 2^{\mathcal{I}}, 2^{\mathcal{U}}, 2^{\mathcal{M}}$ are finite, and $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is equipped with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0})$.

A.1.2 Product $\sigma$-Algebra.

The capability space is

\mathcal{K} = 2^{\mathcal{T}} \times 2^{\mathcal{R}} \times \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \times 2^{\mathcal{I}} \times 2^{\mathcal{U}} \times \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \times 2^{\mathcal{M}}.

We equip $\mathcal{K}$ with the product $\sigma$-algebra

\Sigma_{\mathcal{K}} = 2^{\mathcal{T}} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}) \otimes 2^{\mathcal{R}} \otimes 2^{\mathcal{I}} \otimes 2^{\mathcal{U}} \otimes 2^{\mathbb{N}} \otimes 2^{\mathbb{N}} \otimes 2^{\mathcal{M}}.

A.1.3 Measurability of the Capability Metric.

Lemma A.1. The function $d_{\mathcal{K}} : \mathcal{K} \times \mathcal{K} \to [0, 1]$ defined in Definition 2.2 is $\Sigma_{\mathcal{K}} \otimes \Sigma_{\mathcal{K}}$-measurable.

Proof. Each component distance $d_j$ is continuous with respect to the product topology on its component space:

· Jaccard distance $d_J(A, B) = 1 - |A \cap B| / |A \cup B|$ is a ratio of finite cardinalities, hence continuous on finite power sets equipped with the discrete topology.
· Scalar distance $|x - y| / (|x| + |y| + 1)$ is continuous on $\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^2$.
· Integer distance $|n - m| / (n + m + 1)$ is continuous on $\mathbb{N}^2$ equipped with the discrete topology.

A positive weighted sum of continuous functions is continuous. Continuity implies Borel measurability with respect to the product Borel $\sigma$-algebra. Since each component space is equipped with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, the product $\sigma$-algebra $\Sigma_{\mathcal{K}} \otimes \Sigma_{\mathcal{K}}$ contains all Borel sets of the product topology. Therefore $d_{\mathcal{K}}$ is $\Sigma_{\mathcal{K}} \otimes \Sigma_{\mathcal{K}}$-measurable. $\square$

A.1.4 Pushforward Drift Laws.

Definition A.1 (Capability Kernel). Let $P_0(K_t \mid K_{t-1})$ be the stationary capability kernel and $P_1(K_t \mid K_{t-1})$ the drifting kernel. The drift law under regime $r \in \{0, 1\}$ is the pushforward measure

f_r = (P_r \circ d_{\mathcal{K}})_* \quad \text{on } ([0, 1], \mathcal{B}([0, 1])).

By Lemma A.1, $d_{\mathcal{K}}$ is measurable, so $f_r$ is well-defined for $r \in \{0, 1\}$.

